WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.820
Few people in the entertainment industry of
the '90s had "more power" than Tim Allen.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.400
In addition to a thriving career in stand-up
comedy and roles in movies like The Santa

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.340
Clause, Galaxy Quest, and Toy Story, he starred
on ABC's Home Improvement, one of the most-watched

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.240
TV shows of the decade.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.770
Based on a major element of his comedy act
that men were still essentially ill-equipped

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:24.220
cavemen and had evolved only enough to enjoy
power tools Allen played Tim "The Toolman"

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.220
Taylor.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.560
Tim was a bumbling husband and father, and
host of Tool Time, a Michigan-based home improvement

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.279
show which invariably ended with him breaking
something.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.000
Here's a look behind the scenes of ABC's eight-season
smash.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.680
"Ohhhrahhahrahhaha"

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.690
A tale of two Jills

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:44.229
Patricia Richardson wasn't the first actress
to play the role of Tim Taylor's long-suffering

00:00:44.229 --> 00:00:44.880
wife, Jill.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.820
"Who's that supposed to be?

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.440
Is that supposed to be me?"

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:49.800
"No!

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:50.860
That's not you."

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.320
Actress Frances Fisher originally landed the
role and performed it for the pilot taping.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:00.020
While the inaugural episode was successful
enough to get the show on ABC's schedule,

00:01:00.029 --> 00:01:02.420
test audiences didn't like Fisher in the role.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.049
So, with little time to spare before production
started, the showrunners replaced Fisher with

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:09.210
Richardson, even though it wasn't ideal timing.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.969
"I had just given birth to two babies, and
I really was supposed to do another show for

00:01:13.969 --> 00:01:14.969
ABC.

00:01:14.969 --> 00:01:16.880
And that was supposed to happen nine months
later, so I was going to have all this time

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.179
to lose the weight, and go be with my babies."

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:23.500
The other show was cancelled, though, so they
offered her double salary to star as Jill.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.429
Richardson warned producers that there might
be set delay as a result of her new motherhood.

00:01:27.429 --> 00:01:31.670
"I said 'Look, I'm nursing twins, you have
to wait for me for forty minutes while I'm

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:32.670
doing that.'

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.210
And they said, 'Oh, well, yeah, we'll do anything.'"

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:39.649
And so the new mom started raising her on-screen
kids alongside her real-life babies.

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:41.539
You can (almost) call him Al

00:01:41.539 --> 00:01:45.750
You probably know character actor Stephen
Tobolowsky from… just about everything,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.429
from Groundhog Day to Silicon Valley to The
Goldbergs.

00:01:48.429 --> 00:01:52.289
But the actor could have had a long-lasting
regular gig on Home Improvement if he'd wanted

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:53.289
it.

00:01:53.289 --> 00:01:57.670
Tobolowsky originally won the role of Al Borland,
Tim Taylor's long-suffering, extra-competent

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:58.899
Tool Time assistant.

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:02.369
He walked away, however, when they couldn't
assure him of a proper start date and still

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:04.170
wanted an exclusive contract.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.619
Tobolowsky told Splitsider:

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:09.970
"I couldn't do any other work, any other shows,
which is normal on a regular show.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.209
But my wife was pregnant, we had a baby coming.

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:16.090
I had auditioned for a couple of movies and
was waiting on them, and they said I couldn't

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.090
do movies."

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.440
Rather than risk being broke for months, Tobolowsky
parted ways with Home Improvement, and the

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.130
part of being the butt of every joke went
to Richard Karn instead.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.130
"Whew!

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:28.430
Look at that butt crack you got going there."

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.569
Ashley Judd needs no Improvement

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:35.260
Pamela Anderson's high-profile career really
started when she played Lisa, the "Tool Girl"

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.180
on Tool Time, the show-within-a-show on Home
Improvement.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.120
But award-winning star and activist Ashley
Judd also tried out for the part.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.270
When she auditioned for the show, creator
Matt Williams thought she was "so talented"

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:50.200
even too talented for the somewhat thankless,
eye-candy role of Lisa.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.760
Williams called Judd's agent and said that
while she didn't get the part, he was going

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.770
to create a role just for her as Tim Taylor's
sister.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:01.500
But that offer was declined, and she went
on to star in NBC's drama Sisters instead.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.739
Chappelle's (first) Show

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:07.141
Home Improvement might not seem like the kind
of vehicle to propel the career of one of

00:03:07.141 --> 00:03:10.140
the most incendiary comedians of all time,
but it did.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.980
On a 1995 episode of Home Improvement, Tim
Taylor's Tool Time descended, as it often

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:18.069
did, into a discussion about how marriage
can be tough.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:22.110
Tim asked if anyone in the studio audience
had, like him, been tricked into a fight with

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.500
their significant other.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.090
Two guys named "Dave" and "Jim" could relate,
and Tim pulled them up on stage.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.239
"Well, what were you thinking?"

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:29.701
"I was thinking of calling you."

00:03:29.701 --> 00:03:30.701
"No you weren't."

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:31.340
"Yes I was!"

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.340
"No you weren't!"

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:36.719
As Dave, Chappelle lit up the screen, impressing
ABC executives so much that they commissioned

00:03:36.719 --> 00:03:38.830
a show about the duo called Buddies.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:43.980
It debuted in 1996, with some changes including
the replacement of Jim Breuer with Christopher

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:44.980
Gartin.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.730
Only a few episodes aired before Buddies was
canceled, but landing a primetime network

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:52.590
TV show couldn't have hurt Chappelle's early
exposure efforts.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.590
Emmy snafu

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:58.480
Most TV fans probably don't know that performers
have to submit their own names for consideration

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.610
for the Emmy Awards, and apparently, neither
did Tim Allen.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:05.450
Home Improvement was the actor's first major
small screen role, and he didn't know how

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:06.720
the Emmys worked at first.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.250
When Emmy nominations were released in the
summer of 1992, after Home Improvement's first

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.400
season, Allen wasn't snubbed for Outstanding
Lead Actor in a Comedy Series he just hadn't

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.920
submitted his paperwork.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.860
After somebody told him the rules, Allen made
sure to get his name onto the ballot the next

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:21.860
year.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.270
He was eventually nominated for that year
but lost out to Ted Danson from Cheers.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:29.730
But Allen just couldn't win: his team forgot
to submit his name for consideration the following

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.900
year, much to his disappointment.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.460
"Oh no."

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:34.460
"Oh no."

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.040
"Oh no."

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.040
"Uh-oh."

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.380
"Uh-oh."

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:39.560
"Uh-oh."

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.840
"Not a word you wanna hear in this house."

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:45.440
Tool Time meets game time

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:49.690
Sitcoms aren't easy to turn into video games,
but Home Improvement was so popular during

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:54.890
its run, that a game called Power Tool Pursuit
debuted in 1994 for the Super Nintendo.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:58.890
The plot of the game is that a new line of
power tools by Tool Time sponsor Binford Tools

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.750
has been stolen right before Tim Taylor is
supposed to unveil them on the air.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:06.780
Tim goes on a quest in search of the tools,
using other power tools to defeat enemies

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.380
like dinosaurs, mummies, ghosts, and other
creatures which definitely weren't a part

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.480
of the average Home Improvement episode.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.870
In keeping with the show, which often touted
the dumb, macho, off-the-cuff ways that "real

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:22.250
men" should act, the game's "manual" consisted
entirely of the message: "Real men don't need

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:23.250
instructions."

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.230
"Now there is raw power!"

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:27.900
Whither JTT?

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.760
Home Improvement played to a lot of different
demographics.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:35.140
Adult fans loved Tim Allen's destructive antics,
while many tween girls tuned in for Jonathan

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.380
Taylor Thomas.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.380
"HEH?"

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.130
Of the three young actors playing the Taylor
kids, JTT is the one who became the major

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.130
teen idol.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.760
His face appeared on the cover of all the
major magazines and poster fold-outs, and

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.880
he was the stuff young crushes were made of.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.340
Despite the overnight fame he earned from
the show, though, Thomas left the show in

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.340
1998.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.430
He told People Magazine:

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.190
"I'd been going nonstop since I was eight
years old.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:59.170
I wanted to go to school, to travel, and have
a bit of a break."

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.990
He enrolled at Harvard, studying philosophy
and history… but he also acted in a couple

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:03.990
of movies.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:08.050
That didn't sit well with Allen, who was confused
as to why the young star had left.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:09.100
Allen told TV Guide:

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.340
"He said it was about going to school, but
then he did some films.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:13.340
Did he want to do films?

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:14.850
Did he want to go to school?"

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.170
After Allen confronted Thomas on-set, their
feud became so bad that the young actor opted

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.430
not to return for the series finale.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.960
Thomas and Allen must have patched things
up at some point since then, however, because

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.430
Thomas guested on four episodes of Allen's
sitcom, Last Man Standing, and his appearances

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.820
even included some cheeky references to their
history.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:36.300
"You know, a lot of times, that middle child
ends up being the funny one because he wants

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.300
the attention."

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.640
"Man, you look familiar."

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.080
The season that never was

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.910
Home Improvement wrapped up after eight successful
seasons in 1999 and it was a decision made

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.200
by the cast and crew, rather than ABC.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.580
While he grew sentimental and upset as the
finale neared Allen also regretted not ending

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:56.310
the show a year earlier, when the show was
still a top-five ratings hit.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:01.080
In its final season, Home Improvement fell
to 11th place in Nielsen ratings, but ABC

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.550
wanted to go even longer.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:07.590
The network even reportedly offered Allen
as much as $50 million and Patricia Richardson

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.640
$25 million to return for one more season.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:14.210
Somehow, though, Allen and Richardson managed
to leave that money on the table, and the

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.120
world's time with the toolman was done.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:17.380
Thanks for watching!

